==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|ang|id=turn|inh|gmw-pro>*hwerban>turn}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*hwerban||to turn}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|hwerva}}, {{cog|osx|hwervan}}, {{cog|goh|werban}}, {{cog|non|hverfa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|hweorfan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[turn]], [[change]]
#* {{quote-book|ang|text=''Beorn sċeal ġebīdan, · þonne hē bēot spriceð,<br/>oþþæt collenferð · cunne ġearwe<br/>hwider hreþra ġehyġd · '''hweorfan''' wille.''|t=Man must pause when he tells a promise<br/>until bold spirit would know clearly<br/>where thought of hearts would '''turn'''.|title=The Wanderer|year=10th century}}
# to [[depart]], [[go]], [[proceed]]
# to [[travel]], [[roam]], [[go about]], [[wander]], [[move about]]
# to [[return]]
#* [https://archive.org/details/anglosaxonchroni01thor/page/108/mode/1up The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle]
#*: {{quote|ang|''<small>An. DCCCXIII</small> Hēr Wulfrēd arċebisċeop mid bletsunge Leon þǣs papan '''hwearf''' eft tō his āgnum bisċeopdōme. ⁊ þȳ ġēare ġehergode Eċġbriht cing on Westwēalas frām ēasteweardum ōþ westwearde.''|<small>Year 813</small> In this year Archbishop Wulfred '''returned''' to his own bishopdom with the blessing of Pope Leo [III]. And in that year King Edgebright ravaged the Celts of Cornwall from the east to the west.}}

